WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
The first film showed one coat helping on snow and costing on bare ground.

2
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.800
But does standing out actually change a hare's survival?

3
00:00:12.383 --> 00:00:16.463
Scientists in Montana fitted hares with small radio collars.

4
00:00:17.743 --> 00:00:21.463
Each week, they checked signals and tried to find each animal again.

5
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:24.953
They estimated how white each coat was.

6
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:30.813
They also scored nearby snow and averaged it into a weekly value for each site.

7
00:00:32.553 --> 00:00:36.573
Contrast was the gap between a hare's coat whiteness and its site's weekly snow

8
00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:37.573
cover.

9
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:43.533
A mathematical model then estimated weekly survival at different mismatch levels.

10
00:00:44.773 --> 00:00:48.533
These are estimates for groups, not guarantees for any single hare.

11
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:54.303
When a hare was missed, the model estimated its coat color between observations.

12
00:00:55.743 --> 00:00:59.903
That step adds uncertainty, so the results remain rounded estimates.

13
00:01:01.017 --> 00:01:03.837
Before the estimates appear, predict the direction.

14
00:01:05.237 --> 00:01:10.137
As coat and ground differ more, should estimated survival rise, stay level, or fall?

15
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.180
The estimates fell as mismatch grew.

16
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.960
The fully mismatched estimate was lower than the matched estimate, while partial

17
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.320
mismatch sat between them.

18
00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:28.090
Camouflage is not a shield.

19
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.330
A matched hare may die, and a mismatched hare may survive.

20
00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.470
The estimates change the chance; they do not decide any one hare's fate.

21
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:42.090
A second team tested color more directly with clay mice.

22
00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:46.750
They placed light and dark models on light sand and dark soil.

23
00:01:47.783 --> 00:01:50.463
Bite, beak, and claw marks recorded attacks.

24
00:01:51.843 --> 00:01:55.323
On each ground, the model that mismatched received more attacks.

25
00:01:56.843 --> 00:01:58.563
Color matching mattered in both places.

26
00:01:59.717 --> 00:02:01.157
Add the attack bars.

27
00:02:02.557 --> 00:02:05.577
Most marks landed on models that mismatched their ground.

28
00:02:06.777 --> 00:02:08.537
This held across both habitats.

29
00:02:09.877 --> 00:02:12.237
The pattern follows matching, not one color.

30
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.650
The studies strengthen each other, but neither proves color caused every hare's

31
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:22.870
death. Real predators also use movement, sound, smell, and cover.

32
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.590
Now return to variation.

33
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:31.170
Hares in one population begin changing color at different times.

34
00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.930
Some timing may fit a changing snow season better.

35
00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:40.633
Snow can shift spring molt timing somewhat, but not enough to keep every hare

36
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:45.413
matched. The hares also did not move to snowier resting spots.

37
00:02:46.483 --> 00:02:52.163
If a tendency to turn brown earlier is inherited, hares that have it may survive and

38
00:02:52.163 --> 00:02:58.123
leave more young. Across many generations, that tendency may become more common.

39
00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:05.283
Adaptations help in particular settings; populations change over generations, not

40
00:03:05.283 --> 00:03:10.983
individuals. One hare molts within life, but it does not choose inherited timing.

41
00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:17.553
The evidence links visible mismatch with lower estimated survival in Montana.

42
00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:23.093
Variation gives selection something to act on, if timing is inherited.

43
00:03:24.313 --> 00:03:25.873
The field setting still matters.
